<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">The Ventilation Rule for Indian Open Kitchens</span>The single non-negotiable for any open kitchen in India: <strong>the extractor system must be sized for your cooking intensity, not your kitchen size.</strong> A household that cooks dal tadka and stir-fries daily needs a minimum 1,200 m³/hr extraction capacity with a recirculation filter or external duct. Under-spec your extractor and you will close the kitchen within six months — regardless of how beautiful it looks. Budget ₹25,000–₹80,000 for a quality extractor. Do not compromise here.</div>
<p>06The Material Debate</p>
<h2>Marble vs Tile in the Open Kitchen: The Complete 2026 Guide</h2>
<p>The open kitchen raises the material stakes considerably. Because surfaces are permanently visible from the living space, the decision between marble and tile is not just practical — it is aesthetic and contextual. Every countertop, backsplash, and floor surface becomes part of the home's overall design language.</p>
<p>In 2026, this debate has become more nuanced than ever. The gap between genuine natural marble and the best marble-look porcelain slabs has narrowed dramatically in visual terms — while the performance gap has widened in tile's favour. Here is the most thorough comparison we can give you.</p>
<h3>Marble in the Open Kitchen: The Honest Assessment</h3>
<p>Natural marble in an open kitchen is simultaneously the most beautiful and most challenging surface choice. When it works — book-matched Statuario on a waterfall island, viewed against a backdrop of handleless cabinetry in warm grey — there is genuinely nothing that competes on pure visual impact. The depth of veining, the coolness to touch, and the way marble interacts with light across the day are qualities no manufactured surface has fully replicated.</p>
<p>The challenge for open kitchens specifically: <strong>open kitchens in India receive far more ambient grease, spice, and moisture than closed kitchens.</strong> An extractor handles the direct cooking zone — but the surfaces 2–3 metres away accumulate a thin film of airborne oil over weeks and months. On polished marble, this creates a maintenance cycle that many families find unsustainable. The counter-argument: <strong>leathered or honed marble finishes</strong> (as opposed to polished) are significantly more forgiving, hide etching better, and age more gracefully in active kitchens.</p>
<h3>Tile in the Open Kitchen: The 2026 Renaissance</h3>
<p>In 2026, calling tile the "practical compromise" is genuinely outdated. Large-format sintered stone slabs and premium porcelain panels have arrived at a quality level where they must be considered as first choices on their own merits — not consolation prizes for people who cannot afford marble.</p>
<p><strong>Large-format porcelain slabs</strong> (1600mm × 3200mm is now standard from Italian manufacturers) eliminate grout lines on countertops and backsplashes entirely. A single continuous slab from countertop to ceiling, in a marble-look Calacatta print at 12mm thickness, is visually stunning and utterly maintenance-free. In 2026, this is not a compromise — it is often the design-forward choice.</p>
<p><strong>Sintered stone</strong> (Dekton, Lapitec, Neolith) represents a further evolution: these are engineered surfaces fired at extreme temperatures, resulting in near-zero porosity, extreme scratch resistance, and UV stability. They are the specified material in every high-specification open kitchen project our studio has completed in 2025–2026.</p>
